==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|imploy||obsolete}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪmˈplɔɪ/|/ɛmˈplɔɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-employ.ogg|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-employ.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔɪ|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
{{wikidata lexeme|L5510}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pleḱ-}}
From late {{inh|en|enm|emploien}}, {{m|enm|imploien}}, {{m|enm|emplien||to apply to a specific purpose}}, from {{der|en|xno|emploier}}, {{der|en|fro|emploiier||to entangle, fabricate, to make use of}},<ref name="MED">{{R:MED|id=MED13481|entry=emploien|pos=v}}</ref><ref name="MWO">{{R:MWO}}</ref> ultimately from {{der|en|la|implicō|implicāre|to infold, entangle, involve, engage}}, from {{m|la|in-||in}} + {{m|la|plicō|plicāre|to fold}}. {{doublet|en|imply|implicate}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To retain (someone) as an [[employee#Noun|employee]].
#: {{ux|en|Our company '''employs''' hundreds of people.}}
#* {{RQ:Heyer Unknown Ajax| chapter=1| passage=Charles had not been '''employed''' above six months at Darracott Place, but he was not such a whopstraw as to make the least noise in the performance of his duties when his lordship was out of humour.}}
#* {{RQ:Slate|author=w:Farhad Manjoo|title=BOMBSHELL: Business Insider Kinda Brilliant|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230605073800/https://slate.com/technology/2012/05/business-insider-a-terrific-news-source.html|date=24 May 2012|passage=The site—which was established in its current form in 2009—'''employs''' 60 people and says it gets 12 million visitors a month.}}
# To provide (someone) with a new [[job#Noun|job]]; to [[hire#Verb|hire]].
#: {{ux|en|Yesterday our local garage '''employed''' a new mechanic.}}
# To [[use#Verb|use]] (someone or something) for a [[job#Noun|job]] or [[task#Noun|task]].
#: {{syn|en|apply|use|utilize}}
#: {{ux|en|The burglar '''employed''' a [[jemmy#Noun|jemmy]] to get in.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|act=I|scene=iii|page=313|column=1|passage=Valiant ''Othello'', we muſt straight '''employ''' you, / Againſt the generall Enemy ''Ottoman''.}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Freeholder|issue=29|date=March 30, 1715|passage=This is a day in which the thoughts{{...}}ought to be '''employed''' on serious subjects.}}
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|book=I|chapter=Of Corporations|page=469|passage=As to eleemoſynary corporations, by the [[dotation#Noun|dotation]] the founder and his heirs are of common right the legal viſitors, to ſee that that property is rightly '''employed''', which would otherwiſe have deſcended to the viſitor himſelf: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=w:Gary Younge|title=Hypocrisy lies at the heart of the trial of Bradley Manning|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230517215948/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/jun/02/hypocrisy-lies-at-heart-bradley-manning-trial|date=2 June 2013|passage=Having lectured the Arab world about democracy for years, its collusion in suppressing freedom was undeniable as protesters were met by weaponry and tear gas made in the west, '''employed''' by a military trained by westerners.}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|author=John Schwartz|title=Study Finds Surprising Byproduct of Middle Eastern Conflicts: Cleaner Air|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220616135248/https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/22/science/as-war-sows-chaos-on-ground-it-can-lead-to-cleaner-air-study-says.html|date=22 August 2015|passage=That insight may not seem surprising, given war's dampening effects on economic activity. But the research employed a new tool for recognizing the effects.}}
# To make [[busy#Verb|busy]]; to [[preoccupy#Verb|preoccupy]].
#: {{syn|en|occupy|busy}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|page=171|column=2|act=II|scene=viii|passage=Let it not enter in your minde of loue: / Be merry, and '''imploy''' your chiefeſt thoughts / To courtſhip, and ſuch faire oſtents of loue / As ſhall conueniently become you there;}}
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth Resolution and Independence|passage=I heard the woods, and distant waters, roar; / Or heard them not, as happy as a Boy: / The pleasant season did my heart '''employ''':}}

