===Etymology 2===
{{wikidata lexeme|L320093}}
{{bor+|en|fr|emploi||job, employment}}, the {{deverbal|fr|employer||to put to use, to employ|nocap=1|nocat=1}}, {{etydate|late 17th century|nocap=1}}.<ref name="MWO"/>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The state of being an [[employee#Noun|employee]]; [[employment#Noun|employment]].
#: {{ux|en|The [[school district]] has six thousand teachers in its '''employ'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Kidnapped|passage=“And so you see, sir,” said I, “there is something to be said upon my side; and this gambling is a very poor '''employ''' for gentlefolks. But I am still waiting your opinion.”}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} An [[occupation#Noun|occupation]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|page=162|volume=I|passage=Still he wrote on. He was too much engrossed in his own charmed '''employ''' not to be insensible for a time to all external influences: he might suffer afterwards, but now his mind was his kingdom.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The act of employing someone or [[make use#Verb|making use]] of something; [[employment#Noun|employment]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1833|author={{w|René-Joseph-Hyacinthe Bertin|R. J. Bertin}}|translator=Charles W. Chauncy|title=Treatise on the Diseases of the Heart, and Great Vessels|location=Philadelphia|publisher=Carey, Lea & Blanchard|page={{gbooks|FlpAAQAAMAAJ|24|employ}}|passage=Notwithstanding the '''employ''' of general and local bleeding, blisters, &c., the patient died on the fourth day after entrance.}}

